13: An effective and reliable interaction between humans and machines depends on a proper understanding 14 of their priors. The alignment between their priors is especially critical for communication and 15 learning (Sun et al., 2020). For instance, after a human utters the prompt “go stop go", another human 16 would expect “stop" to be a more likely continuation than “go". On the contrary, after the prompt 17 “ready steady" we would expect “go" to be more likely. The former depends on an abstract pattern 18 intrinsic to the sequence (a simple algorithmic sequence alternating ‘go’ and ‘stop’), while the latter 19 depends on some language pattern extrinsic to the sequence (common use in the English language). 20 Language models are the most representative AI systems extrapolating sequences of symbols, and 21 are very good at exploiting the extrinsic patterns that are borrowed from humans after training 22 over massive natural language corpora. However, it is only recently, with systems such as BERT 23 (Devlin et al., 2018), GPT-2 (Radford et al., 2019), and GPT-3 (Brown et al., 2020), that we see 24 how these models can also discover some intrinsic patterns in the prompts provided. This kind of 25 inference is called ‘few-shot learning’, even if there is no further training or ﬁne-tuning of the model. 26 Many of these language models are based on transformers (attention-based architectures, Vaswani 27 et al. (2017)), which do not feature recursion or loops, unlike some approaches based on recurrent 28 neural networks. Some particularly well-chosen and optimised prompts have shown remarkable 29 extrapolations (Xu et al., 2020; Izacard and Grave, 2020; Hendrycks et al., 2021b), illustrating this 30 new kind of abstraction on the go . However, it is unclear how elaborate these abstractions can be and 31 whether they can even have an algorithmic nature. A more systematic analysis is needed to determine 32 the optimal prompts that make the model capture a range of different patterns. 33 Precisely, machine teaching is the area of artiﬁcial intelligence that focuses on determining the 34 optimal examples or curriculum that a teacher should use to make a learner capture a concept (Zhu, 35 2015). This is of enormous importance today in applications such as digital assistants, where we 36 Submitted to 35th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2021). Do not distribute. would like to teach them new concepts or routines with a few examples, in the same way humans 37 teach or communicate with other humans (Cakmak and Thomaz, 2014; Goodman and Frank, 2016; 38 Degen et al., 2020). It is also fundamental for explainable AI, as the dual problem appears: how a 39 machine can teach (explain) a concept to a human in an efﬁcient way (Khan et al., 2011; Basu and 40 Christensen, 2013). While machine teaching has a long story of theoretical results about what can be 41 taught with a ﬁnite number of examples, it is only recently that the problem has been set in terms of 42 the interactive alignment between the priors of learner and teacher (Goodman and Frank, 2016; Yang 43 and Shafto, 2017; Melo et al., 2018). When dealing with rich representation languages (e.g., regular 44 languages or Turing-complete languages), closer or similar to human language in expressive power, it 45 has recently been shown that strong simplicity priors can make effective teaching possible with very 46 few examples: the overall size of the example set is usually shorter than the algorithmic expression of 47 the concept itself (Telle et al., 2019). For instance, teaching the concept of what an odd number is 48 becomes shorter by using a few examples than by giving the full deﬁnition of what an odd number is. 49 Language models and machine teaching can now meet because of this new few-shot learning ability 50 of language models. We can explore the question of how many examples are needed (or how long they 51 have to be) to teach something to a language model. These are seen as teaching prompts : we want 52 the language model to continue the sequence as if it had captured the pattern that has been expressed 53 in the prompt. From the machine teaching perspective, language models are general systems, as 54 they have not been trained for simplicity or for any particular domain, other than a partial sample of 55 human knowledge expressed as natural language. 56 With their conjunction, we can now ask key questions for AI such as the relevance of simplicity or 57 acquired priors to make learning from a few examples possible (Chater and Vitányi, 2003). In this 58 paper, we examine how a machine teaching setting inspired by Occam’s razor can help us understand 59 how language models ‘learn’ and choose prompts more efﬁciently. We use the term prior ‘distillation’, 60 as opposed to the concept known as knowledge distillation within the ﬁeld of machine learning, as 61 we analyse whether a simplicity prior is acquired as the result of training large language models such 62 as GPT-2 or GPT-3. To complete the picture, we also evaluate some other AI systems (e.g., program 63 induction systems) and humans on the same task, which are known to have this prior. 64 The key contributions of this paper are: 65 • To our knowledge, this is the ﬁrst paper analysing language models in the context of machine 66 teaching. 67 • This is also the ﬁrst paper comparing the acquired priors of language models with humans 68 and program induction systems, when facing patterns that can be taught efﬁciently with a 69 simplicity prior. 70 • We ﬁnd that language models such as GPT-3 have similar or better performance than 71 humans as the number of parameters increase. The performance negatively correlates with 72 the complexity of the concept. 73 • Language models and humans differ in their capability for producing explanations. The 74 length of human explanations correlates with the complexity of the concept. 75 Having a better understanding of language models and its relation to the way other systems, including 76 humans, make inferences is fundamental research that can make the use of these systems safer 77 and more effective, through more robust prompts to solve a variety of tasks. However, this better 78 understanding can also be used maliciously to cunningly modify prompts that alter the results of 79 some applications built on top of language models. A query may fail when using dates, numbers or 80 other strings that have intrinsic patterns. For instance, GPT-3 usually continues “Mary’s father has 5 81 daughters, Lucy, Anne, Alice, Jane and” correctly, but fails on “Mary’s father has 5 daughters, Ba, 82 Baba, Bababa, Babababa and”, systematically continued with ‘Bababababa’. 83 The rest of the paper is organised as follows. In the next section, we present related work in machine 84 teaching, language models and the comparison of machine and human learning. In Section 3, we 85 introduce our theoretical framework, the notion of the teaching size and the importance of the 86 alignment of priors between teacher and learner. Section 4 contains the experimental design, and 87 introduces the agents we consider. Section 5 presents and analyses the results. Finally, we close the 88 paper with a discussion of the implications of this work. 89 2
17: It is only recently that deep language models are starting to show some minimal capabilities for few- 91 shot learning. The abstraction abilities of language models have been empirically shown by systems 92 such as GPT-3 that solve “tasks which require to perform simple on-the-ﬂy computational reasoning, 93 recognize a novel pattern that is unlikely to have occurred in training, or adapt quickly to an unusual 94 task” (Brown et al., 2020). Examples of these tasks are simple arithmetic operations and the induction 95 of symbolic manipulations such as anagrams or reversed words. But, are deep language models just 96 memorising a huge corpus, or do they have the capacity of learning abstract concepts from data? This 97 is still an open debate. On the one hand, recurrent neural networks, such as long-short-term-memory 98 (LSTM) architectures, have shown the capacity of capturing syntax-sensitive generalisations such 99 as long distance number agreement (Lakretz et al., 2019), or language modelling for the game of 100 chess (Toshniwal et al., 2021). From a more general perspective, the abstract reasoning capacity of 101 transformers is shown by Hupkes et al. (2020). On the other hand, there are important limitations of 102 language models on simple generalisation tasks (Bender and Koller, 2020; Marcus and Davis, 2020). 103 The comparison between humans and AI systems has usually been performed with systems that are 104 trained for a task, but not for a variety of tasks that are introduced with a few words or examples. 105 For instance, Kühl et al. (2020) present an experiment that compares the performance of several 106 machine learning algorithms and 44 human participants recognising patterns in 3x3 matrices. The 107 results in this case show that humans, as expected, tend to achieve better performance when few 108 examples are provided. This work highlights that many AI systems are still not ready for a direct and 109 fair comparison with humans when limited examples are given for a range of tasks. Further insight 110 can be obtained when we try to look into the extracted models through explanations, and analyse 111 them in the context of concept complexity. For instance, the relation between complexity of concepts 112 and explanations is discussed by Ai et al. (2021). 113 Regarding language models, Hendrycks et al. (2021b) compare the performance of a text model with 114 humans on 57 tasks including elementary mathematics, US history, law, and more. Only GPT-3 115 improves signiﬁcantly over random chance, but the model is still very far from human performance. 116 Poor performance is also shown for code generation when using text descriptions and a few examples 117 (Hendrycks et al., 2021a). Sinha et al. (2019) show that language models obtain poor robustness and 118 generalisation capabilities when compared to graph neural networks that work with symbolic inputs. 119 The experiments also show that humans (with limited time) performed well in simple problems but 120 the performance decreases with complex examples. Motivated by these limitations, McClelland et al. 121 (2019) propose a modiﬁcation of the language models architecture to extend the ability of these 122 systems to consider context information inspired in the way humans use languages. 123 Machine teaching has been previously used to understand how humans teach machines, comparing a 124 machine teaching setting with a curriculum learning setting (Khan et al., 2011; Basu and Christensen, 125 2013). Many of these studies use feature vector concepts instead of rich representations (Turing- 126 complete) such as natural language. Only recently has machine teaching focused on universal 127 languages, and how relevant simplicity priors become when teaching concepts with very few —or 128 very short— examples (Telle et al., 2019). Machine teaching can determine the minimal set of 129 examples (in number or size) that a learner needs to identify a concept, provided teacher and learner 130 share the same prior. In this paper we propose to do this conversely: by generating examples going 131 from these minimal sets to more redundant sets, we can investigate the priors that the learners have. 132
23:4 Experimental Setting
25: 174 The questions we want to explore experimentally are: 175 • Q1 - Abstraction capability: Is a language model such as GPT able to capture algorithmic 176 patterns from very few examples selected with a simplicity prior? 177 • Q2 - Learning curves: Does accuracy increase from the theoretically minimal witness sets 178 needed for teaching the concept with additional examples? 179 • Q3 - Characteristic curves: How do language models, program induction approaches and 180 humans behave in terms of the complexity of the patterns? 181 • Q4 - Comparison: How do language models compare with humans and program induction 182 approaches in terms of performance curves? 183 • Q5 - Architecture: Apart from being more capable, which has already been shown before, 184 do larger models have a stronger preference for simple concepts than smaller models? 185 • Q6 - Explanations: Can we get explanations from GPT for these algorithmic patterns? Is the 186 accuracy of human explanations related to performance, and their size related to complexity? 187 Before introducing the experimental setting, we present the algorithmic language we will use and the 188 machine teaching algorithm that selects the optimal examples. 189 4
33: We extract eight different concepts c 1 , . . . , c 8 , each c i being randomly selected from all programs in 208 the Teaching Book with i instructions. We bin the concepts into four levels: the programs with 1-2 209 operators (very low complexity, VL-C), those with 3-4 operators (low complexity, L-C), those with 210 5-6 operators (high complexity, H-C) and those with 7-8 operators (very high complexity, VH-C). 211 The minimal witness sets for these concepts in the teaching book are all between 1 and 3 examples. 212 This will be the ﬁrst batch (the witness set, WS) used as training set. As stated before, in this paper 213 we want to test if language models (and other learners, including humans) are capable of capturing 214 patterns when a strong simplicity prior is assumed. As the learner prior might differ from the teacher 215 prior, we want to analyse how learning improves as more examples are provided. Therefore, we will 216 use 5 additional examples in two different batches, a ﬁrst batch with 2 extra examples (additional set 217 one, AS I) and a second batch with 3 extra examples (additional set two, AS II). 218 For the test examples, we generate 5 examples per concept, totalling 8 × 3 × 5 = 120 score values. 219 This number of concepts, batches and test examples aims to ﬁnd a trade-off for a minimally robust 220 evaluation and the limitations of conducting the experiments with humans. 221 With all this, the proposed teaching protocol for each ‘concept lesson’ is divided into three phases. 222 In all cases we ask the learner to complete the test examples. In the ﬁrst phase, we present only the 223 witness set (WS). In the second phase, we also provide the learner the examples of AS I (WS + AS I). 224 Finally, in the third phase, we show all the available examples (WS + AS I + AS II). ,'0'), ('11010','1'), ('0010', '0')} c 4 o+oo If input is '', print '00'; else if input starts with '0', print '001'; else print '1' {('0','011')} {('10','1'),('001','011')} {('00','011'),('0001','011'), ('000','011')} {('01011','011'),('0101','011'),('0010','011'), ('100','1'),('1','1')} 225
37: 226 The following learner families have been considered. 1 227 1 The code and experimental data used in this paper can be found at https://bit.ly/Code3357 5 Humans : We devised a form that was completed by 30 human participants with ages between 18 and 228 54, and 28 of them having university -level education. Apart from the answers to each test example, 229 humans had to write an explanation in natural language for each of the 8 concepts after the third 230 phase. All data collected in the experiments were anonymised, informing the participants of the data 231 treatment and how they could apply their rights, complying with the current data protection laws . 2 232 MagicHaskeller ( MH ): A general-purpose inductive functional programming system based on 233 systematic exhaustive search (Katayama, 2011). It infers programs expressed in Haskell from input- 234 output examples. A strong simplicity prior and a library of Haskell primitives allow MagicHaskeller 235 to solve many problems using only one example. When MH returns more than one hypothesis, we 236 take the ﬁrst one . 237 Louise : An inductive logic programming system based on Top program construction (Patsantzis and 238 Muggleton, 2021). It learns Prolog programs from ﬁnite examples, with the capability of learning 239 recursive programs in polynomial time. Two different approaches are considered, where the left and 240 right recursive calls to the target predicate are omitted (as they end up in inﬁnite loops): 241 • Louise-ens. The most frequent result returned by the totality of the Top program will be 242 used as the ﬁnal answer. Ties are broken randomly. 243 • Louise-exp. The expected score is calculated considering all the possible solutions returned 244 by the Top program. 245 GPT-2 : A transformer-based language model trained on a dataset of 8 million web pages (Radford 246 et al., 2019 ) . The model returns the next predicted word or token considering the previous tokens as 247 the given context. We use the 774M-parameters version of GPT- 2 . The default hyperparameters of 248 the model are preserved, with the exception of the ‘length’ of the output returned, which is modiﬁed 249 to return 3 tokens . By doing so, the experiment execution performance is improved, without asking 250 GPT-2 for more information than what we require for our analysis. Deterministic results can be 251 obtained by setting the ‘top-k’ parameter to 1, but we can get better performance if we use several 252 continuations. Accordingly we will use the default ‘top-k’ value, which is 0. With this, two different 253 approaches will be considered by running the model extrapolations n =40 times for the same query: 254 • GPT-2-ens. The most frequent result (out of the n results obtained for the same query) will 255 be used as the ﬁnal answer. Ties are broken randomly. This approach can be understood as a 256 majority ensemble setting. 257 • GPT-2-exp. Here, the n results for the same query are averaged. This approach can be 258 understood as the expected value for GPT-2 . 259 GPT-3 : The latest approach from the GPT family (Brown et al., 2020). We used different models 260 available, with an increasing number of parameters (Ada, Babbage, Curie and Da Vinci), as recent 261 work (Bender et al., 2021) demonstrate that larger models outperform smaller ones. We want to 262 show whether this trend holds for abstract concepts too, with Occam’s razor emerging more strongly 263 for larger models. We will employ acronyms GPT-3A, GPT-3B, GPT-3C, GPT-3D correspondingly. 264 We performed the experiment considering two different settings: the hyperparameter temperature 265 set to 0 and to 1. When the temperature is set to 1, the results are non-deterministic, and the same 266 approach used in GPT-2-ens is applied. We will use T0 or T1 as shortcuts depending on the value of 267 this parameter. For instance, GPT-3 Da Vinci with temperature 0 will be denoted by GPT-3D-T0. 268 Prompts were designed similarly to GPT-2. Finally, we also asked GPT-3 to provide an explanation 269 of the concepts, just after each batch (8 × 3 explanations). 3 270
41: 271 We take the results of each of the learners in the previous section. For humans, we aggregate the 272 results into a single value per concept, batch and test example, by calculating the average of the 273 30 human subjects. Performance is represented using learning curves and characteristic curves. A 274 learning curve depicts the variation of accuracy according to the number of examples, which in our 275 2 The experiment form used in the human experiments can be found at https://bit.ly/ LearningTest3357 . 3 Complete details of the prompts are included in the supplementary material. 6 Figure presents the average accuracies for seven learning agents shown for the three teaching set batches Interpretation as in Figure 1a Figure b) presents the seven agents shown the four levels Interpretation Figure 1b case is grouped batches WS I II. 276 accuracy C. 277 We start with the analysis for the eight GPT-3 parameter conﬁgurations. The learning curves in Figure 278 1a are clearly increasing in general. The ﬁrst observation is that the systems with the temperature set 279 to 0 obtain better performance than those with the temperature set to 1. There is no clear evidence 280 that larger models in terms of the number of parameters present a better performance for this kind 281 of patterns, which are solely based on simplicity and not other priors. Regarding the characteristic 282 curves in Figure 1b, the GPT-3 systems show a decreasing trend in performance as the complexity 283 increases. Once again, there is no clear pattern in terms of the number of the parameters. Nevertheless, 284 GPT-3D-T0 is the best method, and both GPT-3D versions (the largest models) show their advantage 285 in the very few shot case (WS) and low complexity (VL-C). In other words, more parameters seem to 286 make a difference for easier concepts with fewer examples. 287 Figure 2a shows the learning curves for humans, MH, the two variations for Louise and a selection 288 of GPT methods. GPT-3D-T0 turns out to be the system with the highest average performance for 289 every teaching batch. Louise approaches follow, with a similar performance between them. Humans 290 and GPT-2-ens go next, with similar general performance in the experiment. GPT-2-exp remains 291 slightly under the average performance of the ensemble (majority) approach. Finally, MH is the 292 poorest system, especially for WS, the case with very few examples. For all systems, the accuracy 293 increases as additional examples are provided (AS I and AS II). 294 7 Figure 2b shows the characteristic curves. We see curves cross more often, but overall the order of 295 the learners in terms of performance is similar to the learning curves. The evolution of performance 296 in terms of complexity is generally decreasing, with the best results clearly being for concepts of 297 very low complexity (VL-C). Looking into the results in more detail (see supplementary material). 298 we explain the small increase in the last group (VH-C) as a misalignment between the systems 299 and the teacher, whose representational language is P3. The prior (Occam’s razor) depends on the 300 representational language and the background knowledge in these systems. For instance, if one 301 concept is ‘reverse’ and we have the reverse function in the background knowledge, the program 302 using it is going to be short. As P3 does not have background knowledge, the concept is categorised as 303 complex (it has 8 instructions) even if GPT-3 and Louise, which rely more on background knowledge, 304 can solve it. 305 When looking at the explanations, only those given by MH and humans were minimally acceptable. 306 Despite having tried many kinds of prompts for the GPT systems, as discussed in the supplementary 307 material, we did not get good explanations consistently. For Louise, despite being interpretable, 308 the solutions usually had too many clauses to serve as explanations. As observed in Figure 3a, the 309 percentage of correct natural language explanations decreases as the complexity of the concepts to 310 learn increases. On the other hand, Figure 3b shows how the average size of the correct explanations 311 increases with the complexity of the concepts (size is measured as the number of characters in the 312 natural language explanation given by humans). Remarkably, the average size of the different types 313 of explanations present the opposite behaviour to performance considering the concept complexity 314 bins in Figure 2b. Even for incorrect explanations, the perceived complexity in terms of the size of 315 the explanation inversely corresponds to the accuracy results. 316 Finally, comparing learners by batches of examples or complexity of the concepts as we have seen 317 in the previous plots does not really provide a fully ﬁne-grained metric of how much similar the 318 systems are. This is what we see in Figure 4, showing the error disagreement (proportion of cases 319 where one method is right and the other is wrong, or vice versa) (Figure 4a) and the Phi coefﬁcient 320 (Figure 4b) taking all the examples individually. The matrix for error disagreement strongly depends 321 on performance, so the results for Phi coefﬁcient are more informative. We see that the two Louise 322 approaches have the highest correlation between them. When compared with humans, GPT-3D-T0 323 and Louise-exp are the most similar ones. 324
45: We can now answer the questions we formulated in Section 4. We see that only for easy concepts 326 (VL-C), the simplicity prior leads to example sets that humans, Louise and some GPT models can 327 identify consistently (Q1, Q3). For more complex concepts the behaviour is more erratic, and may 328 depend on spurious factors, as the explanations are incorrect for those very hard cases having some 329 accuracy. The learning curves are increasing for all systems, as more examples are given (Q2). 330 8 The minimal set is rarely sufﬁcient, which means that no system prior is perfectly aligned with the 331 simplicity prior that derives from P3 program size. This is expected as the internal representations of 332 these systems are different. Both humans and GPT have priors to capture both extrinsic and intrinsic 333 patterns, as language models have been trained using natural language texts created by humans. This 334 seems consistent with large language models having a comparable performance to humans. However, 335 this would not explain cases in which GPT-3 outperforms humans, unless (1) our population behaves 336 differently (worse) than the population behind the training corpora used for learning them, and/or 337 (2) GPT-3 has generalised some very abstract latent concepts that are applicable for simple intrinsic 338 patterns, where GPT-3 seems to excel (VL-C). Finally, MH is the system that behaves most like 339 an ideal learner in our setting, but with very limited capability. The difference in the performance 340 between MH and Louise (both being inductive programming systems) might be due to the differences 341 in their background knowledge. 342 A more detailed analysis in terms of correlation of errors shows that GPT-3D-T0 is one of the most 343 similar systems to humans (Q4), so the extra performance is not explained by being more deviated 344 from human priors, but perhaps from higher robustness. When we look at architecture size, we do 345 not see a monotonic relation between results and number of parameters (Q5), but the correlation is 346 clear and GPT-3D, for the two variants that we have studied, achieves the best results consistently. 347 These results may suggest that larger models can be better at capturing patterns that depend on 348 simplicity priors, distilling some kind of Occam’s razor for few-shot inference . However, some other 349 experiments in the supplementary material with concepts including loops suggest that larger models 350 in the future may not be able to solve complex algorithmic concepts, with this distillation being 351 limited to sequential (if-then-else) algorithmic patterns. New paradigms beyond transformers may be 352 needed in the future. 353 Explanations should give us more insight about what these systems are doing. Unfortunately, we 354 were not able to ﬁnd a working prompt so that GPT-3 could generate good explanations for the learnt 355 concepts (Q6). Regarding the explanations provided by humans, the results suggest that the higher the 356 complexity, the higher the size of the correct explanations to cover the learnt concepts. Furthermore, 357 as complexity increases, the percentage of correct explanations decreases. 358 There are some limitations in this work. First, while we use MH as a system that would only be 359 dominated by the simplicity priors, it is not very capable, and it is only useful to disentangle the 360 behaviour for very easy concepts. Louise adds robustness to this analysis but it has more dependency 361 on background knowledge. Second, the use of humans for comparisons limits the number of concepts 362 and test examples that we can do for a reasonable questionnaire, and more concepts were tried only 363 for GPT-3 (see supplementary material). Overall, we think that the machine teaching approach can 364 bring very important insights about what language models can do, what their acquired priors are, and 365 how many examples they need. 366 9
